I'm with you.

00:32:02.000 --> 00:32:03.000
I'll give you the words.

00:32:04.000 --> 00:32:10.000
Then you've got like, for example, the story of the prophet Isaiah has very kind of similar theme.

00:32:10.000 --> 00:32:18.000
He's there kind of deep in like a sort of meditative prayer when suddenly he has this vision of like the glory and beauty of God.

00:32:18.000 --> 00:32:20.000
And he's overwhelmed.

00:32:20.000 --> 00:32:22.000
He says, I'm I'm unclean.

00:32:22.000 --> 00:32:28.000
I'm what he refers to specifically says I'm I have unclean lips, meaning kind of like speech.

00:32:28.000 --> 00:32:30.000
And I live among a people of unclean lips.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:38.000
And then this angelic figure kind of comes down in the vision with this hot coal and touches his lips.

00:32:38.000 --> 00:32:39.000
Right now, there's a vision.

00:32:39.000 --> 00:32:44.000
It's not literally like, you know, it's it's he just has this moment and he sees it in it.

00:32:44.000 --> 00:32:48.000
And the angel says, see, your guilt is gone.

00:32:48.000 --> 00:32:49.000
Your sins are forgiven.

00:32:49.000 --> 00:32:51.000
And the voice of the Lord sounds out.

00:32:51.000 --> 00:32:52.000
Who will go to the people?

00:32:52.000 --> 00:32:53.000
Who will I send?

00:32:53.000 --> 00:32:54.000
Who will go forth for us?

00:32:54.000 --> 00:33:02.000
To which, you know, Isaiah is it's sort of a famous story in the scriptures says, you know, here am I send me, you know,

00:33:02.000 --> 00:33:10.000
and you can kind of can you see some of the kind of themes happening there or take the calling of Mary, mother of Jesus.

00:33:10.000 --> 00:33:19.000
Right. The angel of the Lord appears to her and his first words are, you're highly favored, Mary.

00:33:19.000 --> 00:33:24.000
To which. She responds, not well, of course I am.

00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:31.000
You know, but it says she was strangely or she was troubled at this strange greeting.

00:33:31.000 --> 00:33:34.000
You know, so she's in other words, it's not this kind of confidence.

00:33:34.000 --> 00:33:36.000
Yes, I shall be the mother of God.

00:33:36.000 --> 00:33:40.000
And I was great, you know, but a kind of trepidation and a kind of like, OK.

00:33:40.000 --> 00:33:43.000
And then and then a beautiful, though, kind of surrender to.

00:33:43.000 --> 00:33:46.000
Yes, may it be unto me as you've said.

00:33:46.000 --> 00:33:49.000
Or finally, there's the story of Christ himself.

00:33:49.000 --> 00:33:52.000
We don't have a lot of detail from like his early life.

00:33:52.000 --> 00:33:59.000
There's the story, I guess, of him in the temple teaching and such, you know, but we don't have a lot of like early Jesus stories.

00:33:59.000 --> 00:34:07.000
We do have, though, outside of that kind of gap where birth and then right before his ministry kind of kicks off, he has his baptism.

00:34:07.000 --> 00:34:10.000
Right. And you'll remember the spirit descends.

00:34:10.000 --> 00:34:21.000
And then there's the voice of the one he called father that speaks out and says, this is my beloved son and whom I'm well pleased.

00:34:21.000 --> 00:34:27.000
So can you kind of hear in that some of the themes like it's different, the details are a little different,

00:34:27.000 --> 00:34:32.000
sometimes there's like an angelic figure, sometimes it's just the voice of God directly.

00:34:32.000 --> 00:34:37.000
You know, it's different in its details, but there's a certain kind of pattern here.

00:34:37.000 --> 00:34:45.000
And what I want to kind of contend tonight is that that pattern is not just for the high and mighty,

00:34:45.000 --> 00:34:52.000
and it's not just for Mary, mother of God, or even only Christ himself.

00:34:52.000 --> 00:35:04.000
But that actually that this is kind of our story, that God actually invites all of us into the same process.

00:35:04.000 --> 00:35:08.000
So this is kind of a story I want to tell in three chapters.

00:35:08.000 --> 00:35:13.000
Chapter one is beloved.

00:35:13.000 --> 00:35:19.000
I think it's very important that we hear in this narrative that it that it begins, not ends.

00:35:19.000 --> 00:35:24.000
Right. Not like, oh, you know, you did so good.

00:35:24.000 --> 00:35:27.000
You are my beloved one. No, no.

00:35:27.000 --> 00:35:32.000
Actually, the first word is this word of belovedness.

00:35:33.000 --> 00:35:39.000
We are literally the one who is loved, the beloved.

00:35:39.000 --> 00:35:43.000
As we read a few minutes ago, you hear it there in the calling of Jeremiah, verse five.

00:35:43.000 --> 00:35:48.000
Before I formed you in the womb, I knew you.

00:35:48.000 --> 00:35:52.000
Before you were born, I set you apart.

00:35:52.000 --> 00:35:56.000
I mean, how many amazing things had Jeremiah done at that point?

00:35:56.000 --> 00:35:58.000
Obviously, none.

00:35:58.000 --> 00:36:07.000
This comes back to just our very essence as created in the very image of God.

00:36:07.000 --> 00:36:12.000
You can see it in Mary's story with that language of you're highly favored.

00:36:12.000 --> 00:36:17.000
And of course, there's Jesus. This is my beloved son in whom I'm well pleased.

00:36:17.000 --> 00:36:23.000
Now, again, we're tempted to say like, well, see, but that's Jesus or that's Mary or that's, you know, that's for those folks.

00:36:23.000 --> 00:36:26.000
But what I want you to notice, even in the case of Christ himself, right?

00:36:26.000 --> 00:36:32.000
Again, this happens at the very beginning of his ministry, not the end.

00:36:32.000 --> 00:36:38.000
So before he's done any miracles, before he's done almost anything extraordinary at all,

00:36:38.000 --> 00:36:43.000
the Father speaks over him his belovedness.

00:36:43.000 --> 00:36:50.000
Among modern Christian writers, I can't think of anyone who's written more beautifully about all these themes than Henry Nowen.

00:36:50.000 --> 00:36:55.000
A few weeks back when I was listing books that I was like, oh, and these are good Christian books.

00:36:55.000 --> 00:36:58.000
I'd highly suggest you read. Nowen's was one of them.

00:36:58.000 --> 00:37:02.000
And in his book, Life of the Beloved, he puts it this way.

00:37:02.000 --> 00:37:07.000
He says, first of all, you have to keep unmasking the world about you for what it is.

00:37:07.000 --> 00:37:14.000
Manipulative, controlling, power hungry, and in the long run, destructive.

00:37:14.000 --> 00:37:21.000
The world tells you many lies about who you are and you simply have to be realistic enough to remind yourself of this.

00:37:21.000 --> 00:37:28.000
Every time you feel hurt, offended or rejected, you have to dare to say to yourself,

00:37:28.000 --> 00:37:35.000
these feelings, strong as they may be, are not telling me the truth about myself.

00:37:36.000 --> 00:37:43.000
The truth, even though I cannot feel it right now, is that I am the chosen child of God.

00:37:43.000 --> 00:37:52.000
Precious in God's eyes, called the beloved from all eternity and held safe in an everlasting belief.

00:37:52.000 --> 00:37:56.000
Can I get an amen to that? Like, Nowen, he's preaching there.

00:37:56.000 --> 00:37:59.000
It's beautiful. And by the way, he was a gay man.

00:37:59.000 --> 00:38:04.000
Closeted his whole life. He started to come out to just a few folks towards the very end.

00:38:04.000 --> 00:38:09.000
He's, right, this isn't just abstract theology for him.

00:38:09.000 --> 00:38:11.000
Like, this may be something you're struggling with.

00:38:11.000 --> 00:38:15.000
No, no, this is his own story.

00:38:15.000 --> 00:38:19.000
Struggling with this idea that he is beloved.

00:38:19.000 --> 00:38:22.000
And I think that we've got to come back to this over and over again.

00:38:22.000 --> 00:38:26.000
That really this is, you might call it, our great truth.

00:38:26.000 --> 00:38:30.000
It's the very foundation of our life.

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:40.000
And anything to follow this, any word of correction or challenge or calling or whatever,

00:38:40.000 --> 00:38:49.000
if it doesn't spring kind of from this root of, I am the beloved of God.

00:38:49.000 --> 00:38:54.000
I am loved by God, treasured, chosen.

00:38:54.000 --> 00:39:00.000
If it doesn't spring from that, then no matter what we do, inevitably, it gets weird.

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:03.000
I think often it turns into kind of earning.

00:39:03.000 --> 00:39:06.000
You know, like, Yes, I will. You just watch me.

00:39:06.000 --> 00:39:12.000
I'll be so faithful that surely then I'll be accepted.

00:39:12.000 --> 00:39:17.000
Then I'll measure up. Then I'll be somebody.

00:39:17.000 --> 00:39:21.000
And often we spend decades of our life kind of playing those games,

00:39:21.000 --> 00:39:25.000
realizing that actually we have to start here.

00:39:25.000 --> 00:39:30.000
That we're beloved by God.

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:33.000
It's not something earned, it's given.

00:39:33.000 --> 00:39:35.000
Who am I?

00:39:35.000 --> 00:39:38.000
Who are you?

00:39:38.000 --> 00:39:42.000
You're the chosen child of God, as now it's said.

00:39:42.000 --> 00:39:44.000
Before you were even born, God knew you.

00:39:44.000 --> 00:39:46.000
God made you.

00:39:46.000 --> 00:39:49.000
God sang you into being.

00:39:49.000 --> 00:39:52.000
And you have life and breath right now

00:39:52.000 --> 00:39:56.000
because the Spirit keeps on singing you into being.

00:39:56.000 --> 00:40:00.000
And even if you never accomplish one thing

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:05.000
that the world would call some great success,

00:40:05.000 --> 00:40:08.000
even if that never happens,

00:40:08.000 --> 00:40:14.000
you simply are in your very being, in your existence,

00:40:14.000 --> 00:40:17.000
your identity, the beloved.

00:40:17.000 --> 00:40:19.000
And that's enough.

00:40:19.000 --> 00:40:21.000
So we begin here.

00:40:21.000 --> 00:40:26.000
Of course, easy to preach, easy to say,

00:40:26.000 --> 00:40:29.000
easy to kind of nod along to, like, Yes, all right, point number one,

00:40:29.000 --> 00:40:31.000
I'm the beloved.

00:40:31.000 --> 00:40:33.000
Much harder to live into,

00:40:33.000 --> 00:40:38.000
which leads us to the second chapter in the story of our life,

00:40:38.000 --> 00:40:41.000
self-rejection.

00:40:41.000 --> 00:40:45.000
If only it were so easy that we just had to hear this word of belovedness

00:40:45.000 --> 00:40:48.000
one time and let it sink into the very core of our being.

00:40:48.000 --> 00:40:52.000
And then from there on out, we just live our whole life

00:40:52.000 --> 00:40:56.000
just grounded and rooted, just knowing.

00:40:56.000 --> 00:40:59.000
No other word can kind of mess me up.

00:40:59.000 --> 00:41:03.000
No, no, I know who I am.

00:41:03.000 --> 00:41:08.000
But this is not what happens for us.

00:41:08.000 --> 00:41:10.000
Not that easy.

00:41:10.000 --> 00:41:13.000
Which is why, as I went through all those little narratives

00:41:13.000 --> 00:41:15.000
with Isaiah and Jeremiah,

00:41:15.000 --> 00:41:18.000
inevitably, in each of their cases, all but Christ, interestingly enough,

00:41:18.000 --> 00:41:20.000
they almost all push back.

00:41:20.000 --> 00:41:23.000
So God's like, I mean, take it with Jeremiah, right?

00:41:23.000 --> 00:41:27.000
So verse five is like, Before you, I formed you in the womb, I knew you,

00:41:27.000 --> 00:41:28.000
and all that.

00:41:28.000 --> 00:41:29.000
And then what does he say?

00:41:29.000 --> 00:41:32.000
Verse six, The last sovereign Lord, I said, I don't know how to speak.

00:41:32.000 --> 00:41:34.000
I'm too young.

00:41:34.000 --> 00:41:37.000
Or, of course, Mary, who's troubled, not inspired by the angel's words.

00:41:37.000 --> 00:41:39.000
Or Isaiah with his, I'm undone.

00:41:39.000 --> 00:41:41.000
I'm a sinner. I'm a wicked sinner.

00:41:41.000 --> 00:41:44.000
And we have our own list.

00:41:44.000 --> 00:41:45.000
It's mostly silent.

00:41:45.000 --> 00:41:49.000
I think as adults, we mostly kind of keep it under wraps on the inside.

00:41:49.000 --> 00:41:57.000
But it bleeds through in our actions, in our relentless perfectionism.

00:41:57.000 --> 00:41:59.000
You don't have to raise your hand.

00:41:59.000 --> 00:42:01.000
Any perfectionists in the room?

00:42:01.000 --> 00:42:05.000
Just, I'm going to do it so good.

00:42:05.000 --> 00:42:07.000
So good.

00:42:07.000 --> 00:42:09.000
One day, I'm finally going to get it right.

00:42:09.000 --> 00:42:11.000
And then I will be worthy.

00:42:11.000 --> 00:42:15.000
It's this strange kind of compulsive thing.

00:42:15.000 --> 00:42:19.000
Or our avoidance of anything we might fail at.

00:42:19.000 --> 00:42:25.000
Sometimes it even manifests in a kind of intense criticism of others.

00:42:25.000 --> 00:42:30.000
But rest assured, that person, anytime we get in that mode,

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:34.000
or we see it in others, we're like, Oh my gosh, they're so critical.

00:42:34.000 --> 00:42:41.000
Trust me, they are 100 times more critical of themselves in their own minds.

00:42:41.000 --> 00:42:43.000
It's like the hell.

00:42:43.000 --> 00:42:45.000
You think it's hard being managed by them.

00:42:45.000 --> 00:42:49.000
My God, imagine what it's like to be them.

00:42:49.000 --> 00:42:51.000
It bleeds out.

00:42:51.000 --> 00:42:53.000
It bleeds out.

00:42:53.000 --> 00:42:56.000
I remember speaking with a man at our church.

00:42:56.000 --> 00:42:58.000
It was the previous church, not the table.

00:42:58.000 --> 00:43:01.000
Years back, 10 plus years ago.

00:43:01.000 --> 00:43:07.000
And he was a good man.

00:43:07.000 --> 00:43:11.000
Honestly, very outwardly successful.

00:43:11.000 --> 00:43:14.000
Entrepreneur, started multiple businesses.

00:43:14.000 --> 00:43:17.000
Winning at life.

00:43:17.000 --> 00:43:20.000
And married, beautiful wife.

00:43:20.000 --> 00:43:22.000
And great kids.

00:43:22.000 --> 00:43:24.000
They'd gone through some tough times, kind of in their 20s,

00:43:24.000 --> 00:43:27.000
but then kind of came out of it and were just thriving.

00:43:27.000 --> 00:43:28.000
Doing great.

00:43:28.000 --> 00:43:33.000
And he was really a pillar in the church community.

00:43:33.000 --> 00:43:35.000
Served on teams.

00:43:35.000 --> 00:43:36.000
Lead.

00:43:36.000 --> 00:43:39.000
Just someone you counted on.

00:43:39.000 --> 00:43:41.000
Incredible person.

00:43:41.000 --> 00:43:47.000
But a little bit later, he went through some hard times in one of his businesses.

00:43:47.000 --> 00:43:49.000
It just tanked.

00:43:49.000 --> 00:43:52.000
And it hit him really hard.

00:43:52.000 --> 00:43:53.000
His wife mentioned this to me.

00:43:53.000 --> 00:43:55.000
Hey, could you check in on him?

00:43:55.000 --> 00:44:00.000
So we talked once after church, and then we went out for coffee.

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:02.000
Or no, I went to his house.

00:44:02.000 --> 00:44:04.000
It was a private conversation.

00:44:04.000 --> 00:44:06.000
And I just wanted to check in, see how he was doing.

00:44:06.000 --> 00:44:15.000
And honestly, I guess what I expected was probably some mild depression or something.

00:44:15.000 --> 00:44:17.000
Or kind of like, just down.

00:44:17.000 --> 00:44:18.000
Just hit me.

00:44:18.000 --> 00:44:20.000
I hate to fail.

00:44:20.000 --> 00:44:21.000
Struggling.

00:44:21.000 --> 00:44:23.000
What I found was something entirely different.

00:44:23.000 --> 00:44:28.000
This is what he said.

00:44:28.000 --> 00:44:34.000
He said, Brett, you don't understand my role in life.

00:44:34.000 --> 00:44:36.000
And then this is the thing he said.

00:44:36.000 --> 00:44:38.000
This is the phrase that I for sure remember.

00:44:38.000 --> 00:44:39.000
The rest I'm kind of paraphrasing.

00:44:39.000 --> 00:44:40.000
But this I remember.

00:44:40.000 --> 00:44:41.000
He said, my identity.

00:44:41.000 --> 00:44:42.000
That's what he said.

00:44:42.000 --> 00:44:46.000
My identity is to be a provider.

00:44:46.000 --> 00:44:52.000
And if I cannot do that, I would rather end my life.

00:44:52.000 --> 00:44:54.000
That's what he said.

00:44:54.000 --> 00:44:56.000
And I remember sitting there just kind of stunned.

00:44:56.000 --> 00:45:07.000
It was a moment where you could see how deeply this identity stuff goes for us.

00:45:07.000 --> 00:45:13.000
Kind of all the way down into the roots of our psyches.

00:45:13.000 --> 00:45:15.000
And it's there.

00:45:16.000 --> 00:45:25.000
It's there kind of in the depths that we find that self-rejection kind of lurking.

00:45:25.000 --> 00:45:30.000
We see it in, it can be kind of at times around age and giftedness.

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:32.000
That's a lot of the passages we looked at.

00:45:32.000 --> 00:45:33.000
It's a lot of, I'm too young.

00:45:33.000 --> 00:45:34.000
Or I suck at this.

00:45:34.000 --> 00:45:35.000
I'm not good enough at that.

00:45:35.000 --> 00:45:36.000
I could never be a leader.

00:45:36.000 --> 00:45:38.000
It's a lot of that kind of thing.

00:45:38.000 --> 00:45:40.000
But it's not restricted to that.

00:45:40.000 --> 00:45:43.000
It can be around body image kind of stuff.

00:45:43.000 --> 00:45:46.000
I'm too fat, too skinny, too ugly.

00:45:46.000 --> 00:45:49.000
It can be around intelligence, education.

00:45:49.000 --> 00:45:50.000
I'm stupid.

00:45:50.000 --> 00:45:51.000
I'm an idiot.

00:45:51.000 --> 00:45:52.000
I'm dumb.

00:45:52.000 --> 00:45:59.000
And again, maybe we don't say it out loud, but it's relentlessly kind of in our heads.

00:45:59.000 --> 00:46:02.000
It's there, this self-loathing, this rejection.

00:46:02.000 --> 00:46:04.000
I'm a failure.

00:46:04.000 --> 00:46:05.000
Who could ever want me?

00:46:05.000 --> 00:46:07.000
I'm so unlovable.

00:46:07.000 --> 00:46:10.000
If I died, no one would even care.

00:46:10.000 --> 00:46:11.000
The world would not notice.

00:46:11.000 --> 00:46:12.000
I'm such a screw-up.

00:46:12.000 --> 00:46:13.000
I'm bad.

00:46:13.000 --> 00:46:14.000
I'm worthless.

00:46:14.000 --> 00:46:15.000
I'm a pile of crap.

00:46:15.000 --> 00:46:16.000
What a wreck of a life.

00:46:16.000 --> 00:46:19.000
I'm an embarrassment to my family and my friends.

00:46:19.000 --> 00:46:26.000
See, here we come to the very roots of the problem.

00:46:26.000 --> 00:46:31.000
I think we often zero in on the rejection of others.

00:46:31.000 --> 00:46:35.000
You know, I got fired and I felt so rejected.

00:46:35.000 --> 00:46:38.000
Or that partner left me and I felt so rejected.

00:46:39.000 --> 00:46:43.000
But what I want to kind of focus in on is that the reason I struggle so much with the

00:46:43.000 --> 00:46:49.000
rejection of others is often because I'm already struggling with self-rejection.

00:46:49.000 --> 00:46:55.000
In essence, it feels like what they're saying about me aligns with the story I'm telling

00:46:55.000 --> 00:46:57.000
myself about myself.

00:46:57.000 --> 00:47:02.000
And it just cuts to the bone.

00:47:02.000 --> 00:47:08.000
In other words, if I were really settled in my belovedness, then the opinions, the

00:47:08.000 --> 00:47:16.000
acceptance, or lack thereof, the comparisons to others, wouldn't move me so much.

00:47:16.000 --> 00:47:20.000
The reason I struggle with the rejection of others is because I'm already struggling

00:47:20.000 --> 00:47:22.000
with self-rejection.

00:47:22.000 --> 00:47:28.000
So how do we break out and move forward?

00:47:28.000 --> 00:47:33.000
That brings us to our third chapter in the story, sending.

00:47:33.000 --> 00:47:38.000
In each of the examples that we keep returning to, Isaiah and Mary and Jeremiah and so on,

00:47:38.000 --> 00:47:44.000
there's not really this one moment where they come to see their belovedness and they're

00:47:44.000 --> 00:47:45.000
like, yes!

00:47:45.000 --> 00:47:48.000
And God speaks and they're like, yes and amen!

00:47:48.000 --> 00:47:52.000
And then they go out and they just conquer the world.

00:47:53.000 --> 00:47:58.000
And interestingly though, even though they don't do that, right, even though they always

00:47:58.000 --> 00:48:01.000
as we looked at, they always kind of respond with a certain self-rejection and like, I

00:48:01.000 --> 00:48:04.000
don't know, I'm too old or I'm too this or I'm too young.

00:48:04.000 --> 00:48:13.000
It's interesting that God's response is never like, oh, okay, I didn't see that coming.

00:48:13.000 --> 00:48:15.000
So yeah, well, let's hit the pause button.

00:48:15.000 --> 00:48:17.000
Let's hit the pause button.

00:48:17.000 --> 00:48:19.000
Let's give you a few years.

00:48:19.000 --> 00:48:21.000
You'll get it together.

00:48:21.000 --> 00:48:23.000
You'll get settled in your belovedness.

00:48:23.000 --> 00:48:26.000
And then Gabriel will be back.

00:48:26.000 --> 00:48:28.000
And hopefully you'll have a better answer this time.

00:48:28.000 --> 00:48:30.000
Maybe you're a little more ready.

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:33.000
No, that's not really their response.

00:48:33.000 --> 00:48:38.000
Instead, it's kind of like, that's okay.

00:48:38.000 --> 00:48:48.000
In essence, the answer seems to be each of these people in the midst of their insecurities,

00:48:48.000 --> 00:48:57.000
in the midst of their doubts, in the midst of their fears and self-rejection are in fact sent out.

00:48:57.000 --> 00:49:06.000
In other words, the implication seems to be, I will figure out this belovedness thing as I go.

00:49:06.000 --> 00:49:11.000
Sometimes I have to admit, I have this ridiculous sort of daydream fantasy.

00:49:11.000 --> 00:49:15.000
I don't know what to call it, but like a ridiculous sort of dream or vision for my life.

00:49:15.000 --> 00:49:22.000
And I don't even know if I could actually stand to do it, even if my life circumstances,

00:49:22.000 --> 00:49:26.000
like family and marital status and role here at the table and all that,

00:49:26.000 --> 00:49:29.000
like actually even aligned to do this.

00:49:29.000 --> 00:49:32.000
It's like the most Brett kind of thing.

00:49:32.000 --> 00:49:35.000
So this is kind of my fantasy.

00:49:35.000 --> 00:49:38.000
So I hit the pause button on the table.

00:49:38.000 --> 00:49:41.000
What exactly that means, I don't know, but it's just a daydream.

00:49:41.000 --> 00:49:46.000
So I hit the pause button on the table, and I go off to a monastery for 20 years.

00:49:46.000 --> 00:49:49.000
This is the Brett. This is right. This is where you're like, yeah, I'm Brett.

00:49:49.000 --> 00:49:52.000
For those who know me well, this is classic Brett.

00:49:52.000 --> 00:50:00.000
And I engage silence for hours and hours each day, and I become deeply settled in my belovedness.

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:10.000
And then, like Moses, returning from the mountaintop, I return to all of you.

00:50:10.000 --> 00:50:22.000
And there I pastor you as you really deserve to be pastored by a saint, Saint Brett.

00:50:22.000 --> 00:50:25.000
It's kind of nice to read.

00:50:25.000 --> 00:50:27.000
Saint Brett.

00:50:27.000 --> 00:50:33.000
See, some part of me believes this is what the world needs.

00:50:33.000 --> 00:50:37.000
This is what you all need. This is what my family needs.

00:50:38.000 --> 00:50:42.000
This is what you all deserve. Saint Brett.

00:50:42.000 --> 00:50:47.000
That's the thing. You don't get Saint Brett, do you?

00:50:47.000 --> 00:50:52.000
Some of you are like, no, no we don't.

00:50:52.000 --> 00:50:57.000
You get Brett.

00:50:57.000 --> 00:51:02.000
You get me. Me. As I am today.

00:51:02.000 --> 00:51:05.000
Not even like as I'll be maybe in like 10 years.

00:51:05.000 --> 00:51:09.000
I'm like, no, just Brett, today, here, now.

00:51:09.000 --> 00:51:12.000
As I am.

00:51:12.000 --> 00:51:23.000
With the good and all my crap and insecurities and all the unknowing and not really having a clue.

00:51:23.000 --> 00:51:28.000
That's what you get.

00:51:28.000 --> 00:51:33.000
But you can hear the self-rejection in that.

00:51:33.000 --> 00:51:41.000
Can you relate to it? Not that you want to go off to monastery for 20 years, but can you relate to that self-rejection?

00:51:41.000 --> 00:51:44.000
Can you see it in your own life where it happens?

00:51:44.000 --> 00:51:47.000
Different for each person in a way, but it happens.

00:51:47.000 --> 00:51:52.000
See, I believe Christ is saying to each and every one of you.

00:51:52.000 --> 00:51:56.000
You are my beloved.

00:51:56.000 --> 00:52:00.000
It's easy to believe that for others, like, yes, they are the beloved.

00:52:00.000 --> 00:52:03.000
Or even we, yes, we collective, we are the beloved.

00:52:03.000 --> 00:52:09.000
No, no, you, you are my beloved.

00:52:09.000 --> 00:52:16.000
Behold, just like he told Jeremiah, behold, I'm placing my words in your mouth.

00:52:16.000 --> 00:52:21.000
I'm placing my actions in your heart.

00:52:21.000 --> 00:52:25.000
Go. You are being sent.

00:52:25.000 --> 00:52:28.000
Today.

00:52:28.000 --> 00:52:33.000
Not tomorrow, not in 20 years when you get it all together.

00:52:33.000 --> 00:52:36.000
You're being sent today.

00:52:36.000 --> 00:52:40.000
See, today I'm sending you to nations and kingdoms.

00:52:40.000 --> 00:52:44.000
I'm sending you to your work and your community and your family.

00:52:44.000 --> 00:52:51.000
Just like he told Jeremiah, there you will uproot and tear down all that is evil.

00:52:51.000 --> 00:52:55.000
You will build and plant that which is good.

00:52:55.000 --> 00:52:59.000
Go love the people.

00:52:59.000 --> 00:53:02.000
And to that we say, but I am not ready.

00:53:02.000 --> 00:53:05.000
And Christ says, I know.

00:53:05.000 --> 00:53:06.000
I know.

00:53:06.000 --> 00:53:08.000
But when will I learn of my belovedness?

00:53:08.000 --> 00:53:10.000
How will I get all that worked out?

00:53:10.000 --> 00:53:15.000
And Christ says, you will learn it on the journey.

00:53:15.000 --> 00:53:17.000
All things work together for good.

00:53:17.000 --> 00:53:23.000
Not necessarily that no disasters will befall you, but rather all things will teach you of your belovedness.

00:53:23.000 --> 00:53:28.000
If only you had the ears to hear and the eyes to see.

00:53:28.000 --> 00:53:31.000
The Spanish poet Antonio Machado put it this way.

00:53:31.000 --> 00:53:36.000
Wanderer, your footsteps are the road and nothing more.

00:53:36.000 --> 00:53:39.000
Wanderer, there is no road.

00:53:39.000 --> 00:53:42.000
The road is made by walking.

00:53:42.000 --> 00:53:44.000
By walking one makes the road.

00:53:44.000 --> 00:53:49.000
And upon glancing behind, one sees the path that never will be trod again.

00:53:50.000 --> 00:53:52.000
Wanderer, there is no road.

00:53:52.000 --> 00:53:56.000
Only wakes upon the sea.

00:53:56.000 --> 00:54:00.000
So in closing, you might ask, what do you want me to do?

00:54:00.000 --> 00:54:06.000
Consider this a bit of Christian cognitive behavioral slash spiritual therapy.

00:54:06.000 --> 00:54:10.000
When you notice thoughts of self-rejection arising, just repeat this mantra.

00:54:10.000 --> 00:54:12.000
I'm loved.

00:54:12.000 --> 00:54:14.000
I'm sent.

00:54:14.000 --> 00:54:15.000
I'm loved.

00:54:15.000 --> 00:54:16.000
I'm sent.

00:54:16.000 --> 00:54:20.000
Repeat that any time you notice rejection creeping in.

00:54:20.000 --> 00:54:24.000
May the Lord lead you into the knowledge of your belovedness.

00:54:24.000 --> 00:54:27.000
In the name of the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit.

00:54:27.000 --> 00:54:28.000
Amen.

00:54:32.000 --> 00:54:40.000
So, on the night that Jesus was betrayed, he had one final meal with his disciples.

00:54:40.000 --> 00:54:44.000
And at that meal, he held up a loaf of bread and he broke it.

00:54:44.000 --> 00:54:45.000
He said, take it, eat.

00:54:45.000 --> 00:54:46.000
This is my body.

00:54:46.000 --> 00:54:48.000
It's broken for you.

00:54:48.000 --> 00:54:50.000
And then he held up a cup of wine.

00:54:50.000 --> 00:54:51.000
He said, this is my blood.

00:54:51.000 --> 00:54:53.000
It's shed for you.

00:54:53.000 --> 00:54:57.000
Whenever you eat this bread, whenever you drink this cup, remember me.

00:54:57.000 --> 00:55:05.000
So, as we come forward tonight to participate in communion, as we take in his very body and life,

00:55:05.000 --> 00:55:11.000
may it be a moment of just remembering again, receiving again the truth of our belovedness.

00:55:12.000 --> 00:55:16.000
That greater love has no person in this than they give up their life for their friends.

00:55:16.000 --> 00:55:20.000
And that's what Christ embodied for each and every one of us.

00:55:20.000 --> 00:55:25.000
So, the way we'll participate in communion tonight, we'll just go row by row, starting here at the beginning.

00:55:25.000 --> 00:55:28.000
We'll just form one line here in the middle.

00:55:28.000 --> 00:55:31.000
And we practice what's called an open table.

00:55:31.000 --> 00:55:34.000
Meaning, if you're here tonight and you want to participate, then you're free to do that,

00:55:34.000 --> 00:55:39.000
regardless of your exact, you know, theology or denominational affiliation or whatever.

00:55:39.000 --> 00:55:43.000
If you want to connect with Christ in this way, then you're welcome to come forward.

00:55:43.000 --> 00:55:47.000
You should know the red is wine and the white is grape juice.

00:55:47.000 --> 00:55:50.000
And then finally, you can just partake whenever and however you'd like.

00:55:50.000 --> 00:55:52.000
Some folks like to come forward and kneel.

00:55:52.000 --> 00:55:54.000
Others return to their seats to partake.

00:55:54.000 --> 00:55:58.000
Some just take immediately, but no one will lead you through the exact moment of partaking.

00:55:58.000 --> 00:56:00.000
You can do that on your own.

00:56:00.000 --> 00:56:03.000
So, this is the table.

00:56:03.000 --> 00:56:07.000
Not of the church, but of the Lord.

00:56:07.000 --> 00:56:10.000
And it's made ready for those who love Him and for those who want to love Him more.

00:56:10.000 --> 00:56:12.000
So, come.

00:56:12.000 --> 00:56:16.000
